The recognition index (RI) is used to assess the recognition memory of mice and is calculated as follows:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$ \:{\mathrm{RI}}\:{\text{ = }}\:\frac{{{\mathrm{Time}}\:{\mathrm{exploring}}\:{\mathrm{the}}\:{\mathrm{novel}}\:{\mathrm{object}}}}{{{\mathrm{Time}}\:{\mathrm{exploring}}\:{\mathrm{the}}\:{\mathrm{novel}}\:{\mathrm{object}}\:{\text{ + }}\:{\mathrm{Time}}\:{\mathrm{exploring}}\:{\mathrm{the}}\:{\mathrm{familiar}}\:{\mathrm{object}}}}\: \times \:{\mathrm{100}}\% $$\end{document}

To control for potential spatial bias, the location preference (LP) during the training session was assessed using the formula:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$ \:{\mathrm{LP}}\:{\text{ = }}\:\frac{{{\mathrm{Time}}\:{\mathrm{exploring}}\:{\mathrm{one}}\:{\mathrm{of}}\:{\mathrm{the}}\:{\mathrm{identical}}\:{\mathrm{objects}}}}{{{\mathrm{Time}}\:{\mathrm{exploring}}\:{\mathrm{one}}\:{\mathrm{of}}\:{\mathrm{the}}\:{\mathrm{identical}}\:{\mathrm{objects}}\:{\mathrm{/}}\:{\mathrm{Total}}\:{\mathrm{time}}\:{\mathrm{exploring}}\:{\mathrm{both}}\:{\mathrm{objects}}}}\: \times \:{\mathrm{100}}\% $$\end{document}

For each animal (n = 4 3xTg-AD mice per age group), three non-overlapping fields of view (300 × 300 μm) containing at least one pathological aggregate (either Aβ + or pTau+) were selected from the Ent and HPC for analysis. A glial cell was considered “associated” with a pathological aggregate if its cell soma or primary processes were located within a 50 μm radius from the edge of the aggregate. For each selected field, we manually counted the total number of pathological aggregates (either Aβ plaques or pTau tangles) and the number of those aggregates that had at least one associated glial cell (GFAP + for astrocytes or Iba1 + for microglia) as defined above. The data are presented as the percentage of aggregates associated with glia for each animal, calculated as:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$ \:\frac{{{\mathrm{Number}}\:{\mathrm{of}}\:{\mathrm{aggregates}}\:{\mathrm{with}}\:{\text{ = 1}}\:{\mathrm{associated}}\:{\mathrm{glial}}\:{\mathrm{cell}}}}{{{\mathrm{Total}}\:{\mathrm{number}}\:{\mathrm{of}}\:{\mathrm{aggregates}}\:{\mathrm{in}}\:{\mathrm{the}}\:{\mathrm{field}}}}\: \times \:{\mathrm{100}}\% $$\end{document}

Brain sections were collected from 3xTg-AD mice after the long-term treatments with the same method as described in IHC and were double-stained with anti-PSD-95 and anti-NeuN antibodies. Quantification of hippocampal PSD-95 fluorescence intensity was performed on 5 coronal sections per mouse. For each section, three 150 μm x 150 μm fields were systematically sampled in the HPC. The mean fluorescence intensity from the three sampled fields was calculated for each brain section. Fluorescence intensity was normalized to adjacent white matter regions (PSD-95-negative) using the formula:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$ \:{\mathrm{Normalized}}\:{\mathrm{intensity}}\:{\mathrm{of}}\:{\mathrm{field}}\:{\text{ = }}\:\frac{{{\mathrm{Intensity}}\:{\mathrm{of}}\:{\mathrm{field}}\: - \:{\mathrm{Intensity}}\:{\mathrm{of}}\:{\mathrm{white}}\:{\mathrm{matter}}}}{{{\mathrm{Intensity}}\:{\mathrm{of}}\:{\mathrm{white}}\:{\mathrm{matter}}}}\: \times \:{\mathrm{100}}\% $$\end{document}

The volumes of specific brain regions were estimated using the Cavalieri principle, an unbiased stereological method, on anti-NeuN-immunostained sections (Gundersen et al. 1987; West et al. 1990; Karaca et al. 2020; Sonmez et al. 2010; Altunkaynak et al. 2009). Serial sections were collected systematically in a rostral-to-caudal direction, and every section containing the region of interest within the sampling frame was included in the analysis. The total number of sections analyzed for each region was determined by its rostrocaudal extent, which was identified using the Paxinos and Franklin mouse brain atlas. Images of the selected sections were acquired with a Nikon confocal microscope. The boundaries of each brain region were manually delineated in ImageJ software according to the Paxinos and Franklin mouse brain atlas, and the cross-sectional area was measured after proper spatial calibration (Paxinos and Franklin 2019). The total volume (V) of the target brain region was calculated as:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
